<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<LightningComponentBundle xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <apiVersion>67.0</apiVersion>
    <isExposed>true</isExposed>
    <masterLabel>Market Data Sync</masterLabel>
    <!--
        ⚠ THE description ELEMENT IS CAPPED AT 255 CHARACTERS ON AN LWC BUNDLE, AND THE ONE BELOW
        IS 245. The tag name is spelled out in words here on purpose: an XML comment may legally
        contain a start tag, but it makes every naive length check and every grep match the WRONG
        text, which is how this note was measured at 1,021 characters on its first draft.
        COUNT any replacement with a tool - do not eyeball it. An overflow here has already cost this
        repo a deploy cycle, and the deploy error names the bundle rather than the field.

        The wording earns its length: it names the source vendor pair, the fact that the data is read
        from the PROPERTY rather than from the Opportunity the card sits on (the single most
        surprising thing about this component), the STUB warning, and the one setup step. The STUB
        sentence is not optional - App Builder shows this text to the administrator placing the
        component, and it is the only place the stub is disclosed before the card is on a page.
    -->
    <description>One market-data source (Placer or CoStar) read from the deal's linked Property, with a Last Synced (manual) row and a Sync button. STUB: Sync stamps a timestamp and contacts nothing. Drop one instance per source and set the Data source property.</description>
    <targets>
        <target>lightning__RecordPage</target>
    </targets>
    <targetConfigs>
        <targetConfig targets="lightning__RecordPage">
            <!--
                The ELEMENT form. The `objects=` ATTRIBUTE form is rejected by the Metadata API.

                🔴 RESTRICTING TO Opportunity IS A STRONGER CLAIM SINCE 2026-08-17, NOT A WEAKER ONE.
                It used to hold because every rendered field arrived as an
                @salesforce/schema/Opportunity.* import. The fields are now Property__c imports - but
                the component resolves the record it renders by traversing Opportunity.Property__c,
                and that traversal is HARD-CODED in the JS. So an instance on any other object cannot
                resolve a record at all, let alone render one. Do not relax this to Property__c
                "because the fields live there": on a Property record page the component would find no
                Opportunity.Property__c to read and would render its no-property note forever.
            -->
            <objects>
                <object>Opportunity</object>
            </objects>
            <!--
                🔴 A targetConfig property CANNOT be constrained to an enum, so this default and this
                description are the only declarative guard rails. The component therefore validates
                the value at runtime against its own CONFIG_BY_SOURCE keys and renders a visible
                role="alert" naming the accepted values when it misses - a silently empty card is
                indistinguishable from "this deal has no market data".
            -->
            <property
                name="source"
                type="String"
                label="Data source"
                default="Placer"
                required="true"
                description="Placer or CoStar (case-sensitive). Selects the card title, the Property fields shown and the Last Synced field stamped by Sync."
            />
        </targetConfig>
    </targetConfigs>
</LightningComponentBundle>
